<date date-type="accepted"><day>03</day><month>9</month><year>2025</year></date>
</history><permissions><!--Copyright &#x000a9; 2025 John Wiley & Sons A/S--><copyright-statement content-type="article-copyright">&#x000a9; 2025 The Author(s). <italic toggle="yes">Orthodontics &#x00026; Craniofacial Research</italic> published by John Wiley &#x00026; Sons Ltd.</copyright-statement><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the terms of the <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/">http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ext-link> License, which permits use and distribution in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited, the use is non&#x02010;commercial and no modifications or adaptations are made.</license-p></license></permissions><self-uri content-type="pdf" xlink:href="file:OCR-28-1015.pdf"/><abstract><title>ABSTRACT</title><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0001"><title>Objective</title><p>The aim of this RCT was to analyse the relationship between intermittent vibratory forces and external apical root resorption (EARR) in patients treated with clear aligners, building on prior research on vibrational effects on biomarkers.</p></sec><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0002"><title>Materials and Methods</title><p>A parallel, three&#x02010;arm randomised clinical trial included adults to be treated with clear aligners, randomly assigned by a computerised randomisation list to: Group A (vibration from treatment onset), Group B (vibration after 6&#x02009;weeks), or Group C (no vibration). While patients and orthodontists were aware of group assignments, evaluators remained blinded. Digital orthopantomographs (OPGs) taken at treatment initiation (T0) and completion (T4) were analysed using Linge &#x00026; Linge, Fritz &#x00026; Krieger, and Malmgren methods. Crevicular fluid levels of receptor activator of nuclear factor&#x02010;kappa B ligand (RANKL) and osteoprotegerin (OPG) were measured as bone remodelling markers at different time points (T0, T1, T2, T3, T4).</p></sec><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0003"><title>Results</title><p>Fifteen patients were analysed in Groups A and C, and 14 in Group B. No significant differences in EARR were found between groups, except for a significant increase in Group B (T2&#x02013;T0) using Fritz &#x00026; Krieger correlated with OPG (<italic toggle="no">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.010). Mean EARR was 4.13% (Group A), 4.08% (Group B), and 2.52% (Group C) using Linge &#x00026; Linge; and 3.26%, 2.82%, and 2.12%, respectively, using Fritz &#x00026; Krieger. No adverse effects were observed.</p></sec><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0004"><title>Conclusion</title><p>The effect of intermittent vibratory forces on EARR remains inconclusive, highlighting the need for further research to optimise vibration protocols and understand its impact.</p></sec><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0005"><title>Trial Registration</title><p>ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT05316636</p></sec></abstract><kwd-group kwd-group-type="author-generated"><kwd id="ocr70028-kwd-0001">accelerated tooth movement</kwd><kwd id="ocr70028-kwd-0002">aligners</kwd><kwd id="ocr70028-kwd-0003">root resorption</kwd><kwd id="ocr70028-kwd-0004">vibrational force</kwd></kwd-group><funding-group><award-group id="funding-0001"><funding-source>
<institution-wrap><institution>Spanish Society of Orthodontics (SEDO)</institution></institution-wrap>
</funding-source><award-id>BIJC&#x02010;2017&#x02010;0001</award-id></award-group><award-group id="funding-0002"><funding-source>
<institution-wrap><institution>BIOCRAN (Craniofacial Biology: Orthodontics and Dentofacial Orthopedics) Research Group, Complutense University of Madrid, Madrid, Spain</institution></institution-wrap>
</funding-source></award-group></funding-group><counts><fig-count count="3"/><table-count count="3"/><page-count count="12"/><word-count count="6700"/></counts><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-schema-version-number</meta-name><meta-value>2.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>cover-date</meta-name><meta-value>December 2025</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>details-of-publishers-convertor</meta-name><meta-value>Converter:WILEY_ML3GV2_TO_JATSPMC version:6.6.6 mode:remove_FC converted:11.11.2025</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta><notes><fn-group id="ocr70028-ntgp-0001"><fn fn-type="funding" id="ocr70028-note-0001"><p>
<bold>Funding:</bold> This study was partially funded by the Spanish Society of Orthodontics (SEDO) through a research grant (Grant/Award Number: BIJC&#x02010;2017&#x02010;0001), and the BIOCRAN (Craniofacial Biology: Orthodontics and Dentofacial Orthopaedics) Research Group, Complutense University of Madrid, Madrid, Spain.</p></fn></fn-group></notes></front><body id="ocr70028-body-0001"><sec sec-type="background" id="ocr70028-sec-0006"><label>1</label><title>Background</title><p>Orthodontic tooth movement (OTM) is induced by mechanical stimulation, triggering bone remodelling in the periodontal ligament and alveolar bone, a process initiated by inflammation [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0001" ref-type="bibr">1</xref>]. This inflammatory response involves electrochemical, vascular, and cellular phenomena caused by the forces applied during orthodontic movement. Inflammatory mediators and growth factors play a key role in cellular activation, influencing osteoclast and osteoblast activity during bone remodelling and regulating the rate of tooth movement.</p><p>With the growing number of adult patients seeking orthodontic treatment, the demand for aesthetic options, particularly clear aligners, has significantly increased. Over time, clear aligners have improved in material, gingival margin design, attachments, and auxiliary techniques, enhancing their effectiveness and expanding their indications. Furthermore, patients seek to shorten treatment durations, driven by the desire for reduced risks of gingivitis, cavities, and decalcifications, as well as less impact on facial aesthetics and quality of life. A faster treatment process could also potentially decrease the incidence of root resorption, a common side effect of orthodontic movement linked to inflammatory mediators [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0002" ref-type="bibr">2</xref>, <xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0003" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>].</p><p>External apical root resorption (EARR) is the most common type of root resorption in orthodontic patients, characterised by irreversible loss of the apical root [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>]. Orthodontically induced external apical root resorption (OIEARR) is a frequent complication in patients using orthodontic appliances, resulting from an inflammatory process that breaks down cementum, dentin, and periapical tissue [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0005" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>]. The clinical manifestation and prevalence of OIEARR vary, with rates reaching up to 90%, as assessed through panoramic, CBCT, or periapical radiographs&#x000a0;[<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0004" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>].</p><p>Various techniques have been explored to accelerate tooth movement, including chemical and physical techniques. Physical methods include the application of electromagnetic fields, iontophoresis devices, vibratory stimuli, alveolar decortications, force intensity variations, low&#x02010;intensity lasers, and alveolar osteogenic distraction [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0005" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>, <xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0006" ref-type="bibr">6</xref>, <xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0007" ref-type="bibr">7</xref>, <xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0008" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>, <xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0009" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>, <xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0010" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>, <xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0011" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>]. Two Cochrane systematic reviews assessed surgical and non&#x02010;surgical methods for accelerating tooth movement, concluding that more research is needed and limited evidence exists on the effectiveness of vibratory devices [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0012" ref-type="bibr">12</xref>, <xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0013" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>]. Among these, intermittent vibratory forces are particularly favoured by patients due to ease of use.</p><p>Different vibration devices have been developed to promote OTM. Studies such as those by Nishimura et&#x000a0;al. demonstrated significant results in accelerating tooth movement via vibration, while others, including Woodhouse et&#x000a0;al. and DiBiase et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0008" ref-type="bibr">8</xref>, <xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0014" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0015" ref-type="bibr">15</xref>], found no significant acceleration in OTM. Some studies, like that of Mayama et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0016" ref-type="bibr">16</xref>], reported faster tooth movement without adverse effects, including root resorption, while Yildiz et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0017" ref-type="bibr">17</xref>] found no differences in root resorption between patients using vibration devices during canine retraction.</p><p>Given the growing demand for shorter treatment times, it is crucial to determine whether intermittent vibratory forces can effectively accelerate tooth movement in clear aligner therapy, while also assessing their impact on root resorption as a potential side effect, and understanding their underlying mechanism of action across different aligner change rates.</p><p>The purpose of this randomised clinical trial was to analyse the relationship between intermittent vibratory forces and root resorption found in patients treated with clear aligners, as well as to explore its correlation with inflammatory biomarkers of orthodontic tooth movement. The null hypothesis was that there is no relationship between the use of intermittent vibratory forces, EARR, and the levels of inflammatory biomarkers in the gingival crevicular fluid during OTM.</p></sec><sec sec-type="materials-and-methods" id="ocr70028-sec-0007"><label>2</label><title>Material and Methods</title><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0008"><label>2.1</label><title>Trial Design and Changes After Trial Commencement</title><p>This three&#x02010;arm parallel randomised clinical trial (1:1:1 allocation) was approved by the San Carlos hospital's ethics committee (ref: C.P.&#x02010;C.I: 15/313, ClinicalTrials.gov: NCT05316636) and adhered to the original protocol, following CONSORT reporting guidelines [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0018" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>].</p></sec><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0009"><label>2.2</label><title>Participants, Eligibility Criteria and Setting</title><p>Patients meeting the eligibility criteria were invited to participate in this RCT and provided informed consent. All were treated with clear aligners (Invisalign System) at the Orthodontic Postgraduate Program at the Faculty of Dentistry, Complutense University of Madrid, Spain, or in a private clinic, under the care of the same orthodontist.</p><p>The study included adults between 21 and 50&#x02009;years old undergoing non&#x02010;extraction aligner treatment for malocclusion with at least 14 pairs of aligners. Eligible patients had moderate crowding (&#x0003c;&#x02009;5&#x02009;mm), a moderate curve of Spee, and increased overbite, requiring incisor levelling through intrusion. Exclusion criteria included smoking, poor oral hygiene (plaque index &#x0003e;&#x02009;3), periodontal disease (probing depth &#x02265;&#x02009;4&#x02009;mm), or any systemic or chronic conditions that could affect bone metabolism or inflammation, such as bisphosphonate use.</p><p>All patients included in the study were in the initial active phase of orthodontic treatment using clear aligners, before any refinement stage.</p></sec><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0010"><label>2.3</label><title>Interventions</title><p>All treatments were performed using Invisalign aligners. All participants were required to wear the aligners for at least 22&#x02009;h per day. The orthodontist reviewed treatment simulations for all patients using ClinCheck (Align Technology), with an adjustment rate of 0.25&#x02009;mm per aligner. This force system was consistent with the aligner protocol for bodily tooth movement and intrusion. Intermittent vibratory force was applied with the AcceleDent Aura device (OrthoAccel Technologies, Bellaire, TX) for 20&#x02009;min per day at a frequency of 30&#x02009;Hz and a force of 0.3&#x02009;N.</p><p>Patients were randomly assigned to one of three groups, with aligners changed every 7&#x02009;days during Acceledent use and every 14&#x02009;days when no Acceledent was used (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="ocr70028-fig-0001" ref-type="fig">1</xref>):
<list list-type="bullet" id="ocr70028-list-0001"><list-item id="ocr70028-li-0001"><p>Group A: Acceledent was used from the start of treatment for 6&#x02009;weeks, with aligners changed weekly during this period, followed by biweekly changes until the end of the 18&#x02010;week study.</p></list-item><list-item id="ocr70028-li-0002"><p>Group B: Acceledent was introduced after the first 6&#x02009;weeks and used for the following 6&#x02009;weeks. Aligner changes followed a biweekly schedule for the initial 6&#x02009;weeks, weekly during Acceledent use, and biweekly again from weeks 12 to 18.</p></list-item><list-item id="ocr70028-li-0003"><p>Group C (Control): No Acceledent device was used. Aligners were changed weekly for the first 12&#x02009;weeks and then biweekly from weeks 12 to 18. These two aligner change regimens were selected as reference points for comparison with the intervention groups.</p></list-item></list>
</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="ocr70028-fig-0001"><label>FIGURE 1</label><caption><p>Interventions. Group distribution. Times of sampling/measurements.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="OCR-28-1015-g001" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-1"/></fig><p>Comparisons assessed the effects of vibratory force and aligner change frequency. Group A (vibration with weekly aligner changes) was compared to Group C (weekly changes without vibration) during weeks 0&#x02013;6 to evaluate vibration at treatment onset. Group B (vibration starting at week 6) was compared to Group C during weeks 6&#x02013;12 to determine if vibration was more effective at the beginning (Group A) or after movement had started (Group B). To assess aligner change frequency without vibration, Groups B and C were compared during weeks 0&#x02013;6, when neither used vibration but had different change intervals (biweekly vs. weekly). From weeks 12 to 18, no group used vibration, and all switched aligners biweekly.</p></sec><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0011"><label>2.4</label><title>Outcomes</title><p>The primary outcome of this study was the presence of root resorption in the upper and lower incisors, assessed in millimetres, as a percentage, or using a visual scale. Root resorption was evaluated through digital panoramic radiographs taken at the beginning (T0) and end (T4) of treatment. All radiographs were calibrated and analysed at the tooth level to assess changes in the crown&#x02010;to&#x02010;root ratio, using the Dolphin Orthodontic Imaging software (Chatsworth, CA, USA). Three methods were used to assess EARR (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="ocr70028-fig-0002" ref-type="fig">2</xref>):
<list list-type="bullet" id="ocr70028-list-0002"><list-item id="ocr70028-li-0004"><p>The Linge and Linge method (modified by Brezniak) measures root resorption by using the tooth's crown length as a correction factor to account for variations in radiographic exposure, assuming the crown length remains constant throughout treatment [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0019" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>]. Resorption&#x02009;=&#x02009;(r1 &#x02212; r2)&#x02009;&#x000d7;&#x02009;(C1/C2), where r1 and r2 represent root lengths at T0 and T4, and C1 and C2 represent crown lengths at T0 and T4.</p></list-item><list-item id="ocr70028-li-0005"><p>The Krieger method measures root resorption as a percentage, based on the relationship between vertical root length and crown length [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0020" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>]. The root&#x02010;crown ratio (RCR) is calculated before and after treatment, and the relative change (rRCR) is used to assess root resorption. An rRCR of 100% indicates no change, while a decrease suggests resorption.</p></list-item><list-item id="ocr70028-li-0006"><p>The Malmgren method is a visual grading system classifying resorption into five grades: Grade 0: No visible resorption; Grade 1: Irregular root contour; Grade 2: Apical resorption &#x0003c;&#x02009;2&#x02009;mm; Grade 3: Apical resorption of 2&#x02009;mm to one&#x02010;third of the original root length; Grade 4: Resorption of more than one&#x02010;third of the original root length [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0021" ref-type="bibr">21</xref>].</p></list-item></list>
</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="ocr70028-fig-0002"><label>FIGURE 2</label><caption><p>Methods used for the evaluation of EARR. (a) Linge and Linge modified by Brezniak; (b) Krieger; (c) Malmgrem.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="OCR-28-1015-g002" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-3"/></fig><p>Secondary outcomes included variations in the concentrations of receptor activator of nuclear factor&#x02010;kappa B ligand (RANKL) and osteoprotegerin (OPG) in gingival crevicular fluid at different time points (T0, T1 [4&#x02009;weeks], T2 [6&#x02009;weeks], T3 [12&#x02009;weeks], and T4 [18&#x02009;weeks]). Samples were taken at all time points for groups B and C, while group A samples were collected at T1, T2, and T4. For each patient, crevicular fluid was sampled from the moving incisor at the pressure point identified in the ClinCheck simulation, where the tooth moved in a vestibular direction. Detailed methodology is provided in P&#x000e9;rez Idarraga et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>].</p><p>Additional secondary outcomes included: Silness and L&#x000f6;e plaque index, Modified gingival index, and Bleeding on probing scores (measured in the tooth under study and two adjacent teeth at each time point).</p><p>Clinical assessment of aligner fit was recorded at each visit as a dichotomous outcome for both upper and lower incisors. A gap between the aligner and the incisal edge was noted using magnification glasses, and standardised photographic records were analysed to confirm fit.</p></sec><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0012"><label>2.5</label><title>Sample Size Calculation</title><p>The sample size calculation for this RCT was based on a broader study evaluating biochemical mediators related to tooth movement, which served as the primary variable. Following Kawasaki et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0023" ref-type="bibr">23</xref>], a mean between&#x02010;group difference of 0.41&#x02009;mm in tooth movement over 168&#x02009;h was considered clinically relevant. During this period, mean RANKL values were 70.075&#x02009;pg/&#x003bc;L (SD: 27.6) with force application and 40.625&#x02009;pg/&#x003bc;L (SD: 25.5) without it. Using these data, an effect size of 1.108 was established, and a sample size of 14 patients per group was determined to achieve 80% power at a 5% significance level. To account for a potential 10% loss to follow&#x02010;up, the sample size was increased to 15 patients per group.</p></sec><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0013"><label>2.6</label><title>Randomisation and Blinding</title><p>Patients were randomly assigned to study groups using a computerised randomisation list generated by GraphPad Software Inc. (USA). Allocation concealment was ensured through pre&#x02010;prepared opaque sealed envelopes containing treatment allocation cards, which were opened sequentially by a single researcher. While patients and orthodontists were not blinded to group assignment, evaluators remained blinded, as the groups were indistinguishable.</p></sec><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0014"><label>2.7</label><title>Statistical Methods</title><p>Intraclass correlation coefficients (ICCs) were calculated to assess the intraexaminer reliability of the radiographical measurements. Shapiro&#x02013;Wilk tests were used to assess the normality of the distributions of all quantitative variables. After confirming normal distribution, mean values and standard deviations for EARR were computed and compared using one&#x02010;way ANOVA. Frequency distributions for qualitative outcomes (gender and aligner fit) were reported and compared using Chi&#x02010;square tests. Biomarker data were analysed both within and between groups using repeated measures ANOVA, with time and group as factors, and adjustments were made using the Bonferroni correction. Correlations were calculated using Pearson's correlation coefficients. Statistical analyses were performed using SPSS version 27 (IBM SPSS Inc., Armonk, NY), with significance set at <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02009;&#x02264;&#x02009;0.05.</p></sec></sec><sec sec-type="results" id="ocr70028-sec-0015"><label>3</label><title>Results</title><p>The reproducibility of the EARR assessments was evaluated using the intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC) across the three measurement methods. ICC values ranged from 0.89 to 0.96, indicating high reliability for all methods.</p><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0016"><label>3.1</label><title>Participants</title><p>A total of 45 patients were initially enrolled and randomly assigned to one of the three treatment groups. One patient was lost to follow&#x02010;up, resulting in 44 patients completing the study. An intention&#x02010;to&#x02010;treat (ITT) analysis was conducted to include all initially randomised patients, ensuring that data from the patient lost to follow&#x02010;up were analysed according to their original group assignment (Figure&#x000a0;<xref rid="ocr70028-fig-0003" ref-type="fig">3</xref>).</p><fig position="float" fig-type="FIGURE" id="ocr70028-fig-0003"><label>FIGURE 3</label><caption><p>Flow chart.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="OCR-28-1015-g003" position="anchor" id="jats-graphic-5"/></fig><p>The distribution of test teeth across groups was examined (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="ocr70028-tbl-0001" ref-type="table">1</xref>) and showed no significant differences in tooth type allocation between groups (<italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.206), ensuring a comparable distribution of lower lateral incisors, lower central incisors, and upper central incisors among participants.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="ocr70028-tbl-0001" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 1</label><caption><p>Distribution of tooth type.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" rowspan="2" valign="bottom" colspan="1"/><th align="center" colspan="3" valign="bottom" rowspan="1">Tooth test type</th><th align="center" rowspan="2" valign="bottom" colspan="1">Total</th><th align="center" rowspan="2" valign="bottom" colspan="1">Significance (<italic toggle="yes">p</italic>)</th></tr><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lower lateral incisor</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lower central incisor</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Upper central incisor</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" colspan="6" valign="top" rowspan="1">Group</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" colspan="6" valign="top" rowspan="1">C</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:15%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Count</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">14</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:15%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">% Group</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.00%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">93.30%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6.70%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">100.00%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.206</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:15%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">% Test tooth type</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.00%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">45.20%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">11.10%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">34.10%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" colspan="6" valign="top" rowspan="1">B</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:15%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Count</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">14</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:15%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">% Group</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">14.30%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">57.10%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">28.60%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">100.00%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.206</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:15%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">% Test tooth type</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">50.00%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">25.80%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">44.40%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">31.80%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:10%" colspan="6" valign="top" rowspan="1">A</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:15%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Count</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">9</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:15%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">% Group</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">13.30%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">60.00%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">26.70%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">100.00%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.206</td></tr><tr><td align="left" style="padding-left:15%" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">%Test tooth type</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">50.00%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">29.00%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">44.40%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">34.10%</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></sec><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0017"><label>3.2</label><title>Outcomes</title><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0018"><label>3.2.1</label><title>
<styled-content style="fixed-case" toggle="no">EARR</styled-content> Comparisons</title><p>EARR was assessed using three different methods: Linge &#x00026; Linge, Fritz &#x00026; Krieger, and Malmgren. Comparisons among the three groups revealed no statistically significant differences in the amount of EARR, regardless of the method used (<italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02010;values: Linge &#x00026; Linge&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.238, Fritz &#x00026; Krieger&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.231, Malmgren&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.44) (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="ocr70028-tbl-0002" ref-type="table">2</xref>). When analysing mean EARR percentages, Group A exhibited a mean resorption of 4.13% using the Linge &#x00026; Linge method, 3.26% using the Fritz &#x00026; Krieger method, and a mean Malmgren score of 1.8. Group B showed similar trends, with 4.08% (Linge &#x00026; Linge), 2.82% (Fritz &#x00026; Krieger), and a Malmgren score of 1.43. Group C, the non&#x02010;vibration control group, demonstrated the lowest EARR values (2.52%, 2.12%, and 1.27%, respectively). While EARR values were slightly higher in the vibration groups, the differences were not statistically significant, indicating no measurable effect of vibration on root resorption.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="ocr70028-tbl-0002" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 2</label><caption><p>Comparison of EARR measurements across groups using three different assessment methods.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" colspan="9" valign="bottom" rowspan="1">Descriptives</th></tr><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" rowspan="2" valign="bottom" colspan="1">Method</th><th align="center" rowspan="2" valign="bottom" colspan="1">Group</th><th align="center" rowspan="2" valign="bottom" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">N</italic>
</th><th align="center" rowspan="2" valign="bottom" colspan="1">Mean</th><th align="center" colspan="2" valign="bottom" rowspan="1">95% Confidence</th><th align="center" rowspan="2" valign="bottom" colspan="1">Minimum</th><th align="center" rowspan="2" valign="bottom" colspan="1">Maximum</th><th align="center" rowspan="2" valign="bottom" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">p</italic>
</th></tr><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lower bound</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Upper bound</th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" rowspan="3" valign="top" colspan="1">Linge &#x00026; Linge (%)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">C</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.52</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.86</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.17</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">7.97</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;1.18</td><td align="center" rowspan="3" valign="top" colspan="1">0.238</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">B</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">14</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.08</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.43</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.72</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">8.26</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.93</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.13</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6.17</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.09</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">10.74</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.17</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="3" valign="top" colspan="1">Fritz Krieger (%)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">C</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.12</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.07</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.16</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.74</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x02212;0.89</td><td align="center" rowspan="3" valign="top" colspan="1">0.231</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">B</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">14</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.82</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.44</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.21</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5.51</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.44</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3.26</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.57</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.95</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">6.97</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.16</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="3" valign="top" colspan="1">Malmgrem (1&#x02013;5 scale)</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">C</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.27</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.73</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.8</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td><td align="center" rowspan="3" valign="top" colspan="1">0.44</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">B</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">14</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.43</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0.84</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.02</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">3</td></tr><tr><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.8</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">1.01</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">2.59</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">0</td><td align="center" valign="top" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></sec><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0019"><label>3.2.2</label><title>Variations in <styled-content style="fixed-case" toggle="no">RANKL</styled-content> and <styled-content style="fixed-case" toggle="no">OPG</styled-content> Concentrations in Gingival Crevicular Fluid</title><p>Biomarkers concentrations were assessed at different time points (T0, T1, T2, T3, and T4). Mixed&#x02010;model ANOVA revealed no significant differences in RANKL (Group A: <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.31, Group B: <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.8, Group C: <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.49) or OPG (Group A: <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.24, Group B: <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.58, Group C: <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.59) over time or in relation to the application of vibration or the frequency of aligner changes. However, a nonsignificant improvement in biomarker concentrations was observed when aligners were changed every 7&#x02009;days with vibration applied. Additionally, no statistically significant differences were found between or within groups for the plaque index, gingival index, or bleeding on probing scores. These results are further detailed in P&#x000e9;rez Idarraga et&#x000a0;al. [<xref rid="ocr70028-bib-0022" ref-type="bibr">22</xref>].</p></sec><sec id="ocr70028-sec-0020"><label>3.2.3</label><title>Correlations of <styled-content style="fixed-case" toggle="no">EARR</styled-content> With Biomarker Concentrations</title><p>Correlations between EARR and the inflammatory biomarkers RANKL and OPG at different time points were assessed (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="ocr70028-tbl-0003" ref-type="table">3</xref>). In Group B, a significant negative correlation was found between EARR measured at T2 to T0 using the Fritz &#x00026; Krieger method and OPG concentrations (<italic toggle="yes">R</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;&#x02212;0.684; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.01), with a trend toward significance when using the Linge &#x00026; Linge method (<italic toggle="yes">R</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;&#x02212;0.503; <italic toggle="yes">p</italic>&#x02009;=&#x02009;0.08). No significant correlations were observed in Groups A and C. For the remaining comparisons, no significant associations were found between biomarker levels at different time points and the extent of root resorption across the groups.</p><table-wrap position="float" id="ocr70028-tbl-0003" content-type="TABLE"><label>TABLE 3</label><caption><p>Correlation between EARR and biomarker levels across groups.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><thead valign="bottom"><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" colspan="7" valign="bottom" rowspan="1">Correlations</th></tr><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="left" rowspan="2" valign="bottom" colspan="1">Group</th><th align="center" rowspan="2" valign="bottom" colspan="1">Method</th><th align="center" rowspan="2" valign="bottom" colspan="1">Markers</th><th align="center" colspan="4" valign="bottom" rowspan="1">Statistic</th></tr><tr style="border-bottom:solid 1px #000000"><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Correlation</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lower CI</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Upper CI</th><th align="center" valign="bottom" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">p</italic>
